The refactor: extract a shared helper `digest_file(path: &Path) -> Result<Sha256Digest, ...>` in main.rs that opens the file, reads it, and produces the error message (or a typed error distinguishing open vs read). Then `run_digest` and `run_check_digest` both use it.

Design: return `Result<Sha256Digest, String>` where the String is the full error message line (without trailing newline?) — or return an enum. Simpler: helper returns `Result<Sha256Digest, String>` with the message body like `inkseal: cannot open '{display}': {err}` and callers do `emit_error(1, format!("{msg}\n").as_bytes())`. Or helper itself emits and returns ExitCode... Better to keep the emit in callers? Actually the cleanest: helper returns the digest or the formatted error message; both callers then call emit_error(1, ...). Or the helper could directly return `Result<Sha256Digest, ExitCode>` — where Err carries the ExitCode after emitting. That's a common pattern: 

```rust
fn digest_file(path: &Path) -> Result<Sha256Digest, ExitCode> {
    let display = path_display(path);
    let file = File::open(path).map_err(|err| {
        emit_error(1, format!("inkseal: cannot open '{display}': {err}\n").as_bytes())
    })?;
    inkseal::digest_reader(file).map_err(|err| {
        emit_error(1, format!("inkseal: cannot read '{display}': {err}\n").as_bytes())
    })
}
```

Then run_digest:

```rust
fn run_digest(path: &Path) -> ExitCode {
    match digest_file(path) {
        Ok(digest) => { let mut line = digest.to_hex(); line.push('\n'); emit_success(line.as_bytes()) }
        Err(code) => code,
    }
}
```

run_check_digest:

```rust
let actual = match digest_file(path) { Ok(d) => d, Err(code) => return code };
```

That preserves all behavior. Add a doc comment explaining the unified rule. Write the edit.

